<p>The White House has warned staff not to engage in insider trading or place bets on prediction markets as scrutiny grows over suspicious trading tied to President Trump&#x27;s decisions during the Iran conflict. The guidance came after reports of unusually well-timed positions in prediction markets, oil futures, and stocks, including bets linked to a possible U.S.-Iran ceasefire. Lawmakers are now calling for investigations after The Globe and Mail reported that at least 50 new accounts placed substantial Polymarket bets on an Iran ceasefire hours before President Trump announced it. The story centers on possible misuse of sensitive information, but the new warning also shows officials trying to tighten internal guardrails around conflicts of interest as prediction markets become more visible in political and geopolitical events.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/eI24DPYgWk9klvtokst7U_wOBpmWKuF7w3xAUB0I4TqF-_u746ZbrYA8zqcSNEjl_HIIiTrkY9rkgZd_g-_VyLBDldL8aT9tJ7eNk7hH7EzTU8Aui7EY72JW9p8jMZXMAqMnKTEjmHiqixutODnm3X4vk0IoCFhb-Mb444qIXloni58cPY9gArAgF0d0JqXYe3SQMjDCZ7_1vq67hWgT3S_Sudkm9OZXOtd67ckV3hG7JB2d-QBjAj16UdqVK6FLXKeQ3vxD56BCp-XAgQ' alt='Rep. Ritchie Torres speaks at a Capitol Hill news conference in Washington, D.C., in July 2023.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>A cross-market footprint: The Straits Times said the suspicious activity showed up in prediction markets, oil futures, and stocks, suggesting that any leak of policy information could ripple across several venues at once.</li><li>Why prediction markets were singled out: BBC said these platforms have grown popular enough for users to wager directly on conflicts, making them a natural target for internal ethics guidance.</li><li>The stakes are rising: Lawmakers&#x27; calls for investigations mean the matter is no longer just an internal White House compliance question.</li><li>A pattern, not a one-off: NDTV described the trigger as a series of recent, well-timed, profitable bets linked to President Trump&#x27;s military decision-making.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: The administration&#x27;s message was preventive. Staff were reminded that using sensitive policy information for trades and placing bets on prediction markets both fall outside acceptable conduct as scrutiny intensified. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgld65x396go'>BBC</a>)</li><li>U.S. lawmakers: Lawmakers saw the ceasefire-linked wagers as serious enough to warrant outside scrutiny, arguing that the trading pattern should be examined rather than handled only as an internal ethics matter. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-us-lawmakers-investigations-polymarket-bets-iran-war-ceasefire/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Media analysis: Coverage in outlets such as The Straits Times framed the episode as a broader market-surveillance problem because unusual activity appeared in betting markets, oil futures, and equities at roughly the same time. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/white-house-warned-staff-not-to-engage-in-insider-trading-amid-war-with-iran'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/white-house-warns-staff-on-insider-trading-amid-iran-war-bets-11336874#publisher=newsstand'>White House Warns Staff On Insider Trading Amid Iran War Bets</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/white-house-warned-staff-not-to-engage-in-insider-trading-amid-war-with-iran'>White House warned staff not to engage in insider trading amid war with Iran</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgld65x396go'>White House staff told not to place bets on prediction markets</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-us-lawmakers-investigations-polymarket-bets-iran-war-ceasefire/'>U.S. lawmakers call for investigations after well-timed Polymarket bets on Iran ceasefire</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li></ul>